# 45. PERSISTENCE REMOVAL

During incident response:

```text
Identify
   ↓
Validate
   ↓
Contain
   ↓
Remove persistence
   ↓
Remove associated malware
   ↓
Reset compromised credentials
   ↓
Patch root cause
   ↓
Verify
   ↓
Monitor
```

Do not simply delete one suspicious file and assume the incident is over.

---

# 46. WHY REMOVING ONE MECHANISM MAY NOT BE ENOUGH

An attacker could have multiple persistence mechanisms.

For example:

```text
Scheduled Task
      +
Service
      +
Compromised Account
```

Removing only the scheduled task leaves:

```text
Service
+
Compromised Account
```

Therefore:

> **Incident response must search for multiple persistence mechanisms.**

---

# 47. CREDENTIAL RESET

If credentials may have been compromised:

```text
Identify compromised credentials
          ↓
Disable/revoke
          ↓
Reset
          ↓
Revoke sessions/tokens where appropriate
          ↓
Review authentication logs
```

Changing a password alone may not remove:

- Existing sessions
- API tokens
- SSH keys
- Service credentials
- Application credentials

---

# 48. PERSISTENCE AND LEAST PRIVILEGE

Least privilege reduces persistence impact.

If a compromised account has minimal permissions:

```text
Compromise
   ↓
Limited privileges
   ↓
Reduced impact
```

If it has excessive privileges:

```text
Compromise
   ↓
Administrative access
   ↓
Greater persistence possibilities
```

---

